WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.570
Every time you like a photo, an app saves

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.110
who liked it, which photo, and when.

3
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.360
Millions of times a second,

4
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.880
for millions of people.

5
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:11.430
Not in a spreadsheet!

6
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.560
Today: how apps store data in

7
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.880
databases, with tables, keys and types.

8
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.380
A database keeps the tables for an app.

9
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.260
Many users read and write at once, safely.

10
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.470
Every column has a type, and rules

11
00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.330
stop bad data at the door.

12
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.230
And every fact is stored once.

13
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.370
Each table has a primary key: a

14
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.430
column whose value is different in

15
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.510
every row, like a user id.

16
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.540
Names and birthdays make bad keys,

17
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.760
because two people can share them.

18
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.040
Tables link with foreign keys.

19
00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.850
A likes table holds a user

20
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.950
id and a photo id.

21
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.400
Each one points to a row in another table.

22
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.970
Designing those tables is called a

23
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.510
schema: the tables, their columns

24
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.770
and types, and how they link.

25
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.810
A good schema stores each fact

26
00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.530
in exactly one place.

27
00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:14.390
Quick check!

28
00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.210
Which column makes the best primary

29
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.850
key for a school's pupils?

30
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.220
A pupil number, given once

31
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.540
and never reused.

32
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.080
Names and birthdays repeat, and

33
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.220
names change spelling.

34
00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.670
Then you ask questions in SQL.

35
00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:35.190
SELECT picks the columns, FROM the

36
00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.330
table, and WHERE the rows.

37
00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.850
It works the same on twelve

38
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.410
rows or twelve million.

39
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.780
Store a fact twice and the

40
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:45.980
copies drift apart.

41
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.580
Change a teacher's phone in one

42
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.100
row, miss five others, and the

43
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.020
database tells two stories.

44
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.570
In the app, split a wide school

45
00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.610
table into two, then three.

46
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.070
The repeated facts fall from

47
00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.930
thirty-two to eight to zero.

48
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.620
Then try a hurried change on each design.

49
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:09.830
Your turn.

50
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.730
Why store the class teacher's name in a

51
00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:17.850
classes table, and not in every pupil's

52
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:18.090
row?

53
00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.600
So a change happens in one

54
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.820
place, and every row agrees.

55
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.040
Remember: a primary key picks out one row.

56
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.140
Foreign keys link tables.

57
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.810
And a good schema stores each fact once.

58
00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.490
Try it tomorrow: pick an app you

59
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.290
use, like a messaging app.

60
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.450
Sketch its tables: what is a row

61
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.050
in each, and what's the key?

62
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.820
Which tables link?
